("sub", "9.9 What the objections leave"),
("p", "The objections do not overturn the thesis. Words of destruction can name an ending, and they also name the ruin of persons who go on existing, so they do not decide the question. The adjective of duration establishes permanence, and the passages about the persons say what the permanence is. The picture of the fire and the worm is a picture of corpses in Isaiah and of a casting of the living in the Gospels. The smoke may be a monument, and the persons beside it are tormented and have no rest day nor night. What remains when every concession is made is the statement of Revelation about persons who are tormented, who have no rest day nor night, and whose plural statement of torment at Revelation 20:10 takes in the beast and the false prophet cast in a thousand years before. That remains because it is in the text, and nothing in the objections has removed it."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Five writers who hold that the lost cease to exist are named, and three who answer them, with a survey of the debate; modern writers are quoted only where their words were read in a free full text.",
    "The words of destruction can name an ending and also the ruin of persons who live; the passage, not the word, decides.",
    "The adjective “eternal” establishes permanence; the passages about the persons say what the punishment is.",
    "The fire and the worm and the smoke are conceded where the Old Testament uses them of a finished ruin, and answered from the Lord’s casting of living persons into the fire and from the sentences about persons in Revelation.",
    "The objection from justice is answered from what Scripture says of the Judge and of the grading of judgment, and not from a rule of proportion that Scripture does not state.",
    "The objection from the earthly judgments of the wicked is answered by the settings of the passages, which speak of the living on the earth.",
  ]),
